    "result_text": "All tests pass \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**, 30 tests, 0 failures.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the daily owner-creation limit rule following the existing patterns in the codebase (mirroring the `CityAtCapacityException` / per-city-cap rule).\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`DailyOwnerLimitExceededException.java`** (new) \u2014 a `RuntimeException` carrying the offending `LocalDate`, thrown when the day's limit is reached.\n\n2. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice.java`** \u2014 added an `@ExceptionHandler` that maps the new exception to **HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests)** with a `ProblemDetail` body, consistent with the other owner-rule handlers.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** (the `POST /api/owners` handler) \u2014 after required-field validation and before the city-capacity check, it counts existing owners whose `registrationDate` equals today (`LocalDate.now()`) and throws `DailyOwnerLimitExceededException` when that count is `>= 100` (new constant `MAX_OWNERS_PER_DAY = 100`, plus a `countOwnersRegisteredOn` helper).\n\nSince owners default their `registrationDate` to the current date when none is supplied, creating 100 owners today fills the quota and the 101st is rejected with 429 \u2014 exactly what `Cp19Tests.coreRejectsOverDailyLimit` asserts. The full acceptance suite remains green.",
